{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the evidence-rules first slice at head 9c15879a71dc761dee65d15b29c833cf5175605f (tree 701cd05e), for request a53e1464, builder promise a891555c and review request 3e675acb. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nThe slice keeps every ruling it rests on. Gate 3 passed at the code checkpoint, and the evidence seal verifies file by file. Four things stop the landing. The first is a security defect: a second key of the checker member can strand a room job. The other three are in the documents. The implementation note does not meet the house writing rule, the design note's status table still calls built parts open, and one sentence claims a deployment authorization without naming it.\n\nI read the implementation note at the head first. Then I read the diffs from main 8659af46 to the head, the four rulings and the design note. I had one independent read of the mark, admission and signing path. I checked its main finding against the code myself.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. The head merges main 8659af46 and is 84 commits over it, with 131 changed paths.\n- Gate 3 ran `npm run gate` at 3713cff17e (tree 5a5d1c11) and exited 0: Node 78, Counting 55, plain 3,090 passed and 1 skipped, Native 836 passed and 2 skipped. The receipt's `postHead` equals its head.\n- Gates 1 and 2 are failed rows: exit 1 at 26315118 and at dcd80f3d.\n- From 3713cff1 to the head, two paths change: the implementation note and the manifest.\n- The two regenerated modules are unchanged from f5031122 to the head. Their sha256 values at the head are the ones the note gives: `56f2c2bf…` for packages/mcp/src/views/generated.ts and `994fb121…` for apps/room/page/assets.ts.\n- The committed manifest's sha256 is `f3e3c6cf…4903a1`, as the request says.\n- I hashed every file in the manifest. All 27,220 match by size and sha256, 973,595,305 bytes in all. The directory has no file outside the list and no writable file. The root SHA256SUMS checks clean: 27,219 lines, no failure.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 736 links, 0 broken.\n- check-commit-messages: 84 checked, 0 flagged.\n- check-notes: 1 warning, the 4,756,047-byte manifest.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Against the rulings\n\n- **393b3b99, capacity.** destination@17 declares no `whole-scope` capacity, so `turn.ts` `#full` takes the legacy `fits` path. The head removes the account-settlement and publication-settlement modules (403 lines) and witness N9. The count guards are in `EVIDENCE_LIMITS_V17`: 2,048 keys, 8 candidates, 512 holds, 512 generations, 8,192 incidents and 32 triagers. The note carries the destination@15 table and the discarded destination@17 table, each labelled as compiled declarations. docs/architecture.md says which paths the kept carriers serve (published, audit, result and incident) and that they make no byte guarantee. N5 runs the five published jobs under legacy admission.\n- **1bc4f7fd, kernel scope.** Rules observation revision 21 takes the same form as revisions 19 and 20 in bytes/records.ts, contract/observation.ts and derive/observes.ts. `contentChecked` requires the two fields exactly when the definition declares `roomServiceChanged`. The contract gains only the type-only `ServiceMark` and `ScopedRulesPosition`. packages/bytes/src/evidence.ts is gone, and no kernel package names `EvidenceBinding`, `CheckSource`, `CheckStage` or `CheckSubject`.\n- **c20152ef, timeout seam.** destination@17 adds no timed port. The design note's Holds section has the timeout sentence and names the ruling. These tests cover witnesses (a) to (e):\n  - N6 (`literal native TIME…`) covers (a). The landing admitted before the deadline stays admitted. The landing after the timed entry is refused `extent-regressed`, and the effective hold names the timed-out job. The timed entry changes only its job and sends nothing.\n  - N6 also covers (b): it reads the fold back with `verify(…, { mode: 'replay' })`.\n  - evidence-timed-v17.test.ts covers (c), with `timed-partial`.\n  - N6 covers (d). The fourth job is refused `evidence-funding-unavailable`, and no job opens.\n  - evidence-holds-v17.test.ts covers (e).\n- **f73805e4, incidents.** The stage table's `published` row names the nested incident Issue for active `issue.triage` holders.\n- **31962e1f, validation.** All five conditions hold. The note names `node packages/mcp/scripts/views.mjs` and `npm run build --workspace @generalbusiness/dap-page`. It says what made each changed assertion stale: Rules 13 is now registered, and the pure subpath exports `isObjectId`. The test diffs keep every original case.\n- **History.** Each change to existing Destination code takes effect only behind `successorOf(given)`, which only `destinationRulesForV17` attaches, and that call refuses any pin but destination@17. Older pins take the same branches as before. creation-versions.ts and the Workers' settings do not change, so the cohort is opt-in and new rooms keep today's defaults.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. **A second key of the checker member strands a room job (Security).**\n   - The Change and Issue 4 lanes accept a `check` act from any key of the job's checker member. The guard is `{ signer: [\"checker\"], of: \"also.job\" }` (apps/room/lanes/src/evidence-fields.ts:63).\n   - The first accepted result moves the lane job from `requested` to `passed`, `failed` or `errored` and sets `decidedBy` (the effects at lines 51 and 54).\n   - Destination 17 refuses that result with `wrong-source` when its key is not the marked key (destination-evidence-v17.ts:1198). Its job record stays `admitted`, and a published job takes a hold (the `refuse` path at :1183).\n   - The lane's `check-decision` handler does not reopen the job after a refused result.\n   - The marked service's own result comes second. It writes no state effect on the lane job, so `readEvidenceDecisionV17` returns null (evidence-decision-v17.ts:37), and the destination refuses it `not-this-check`.\n   - So a landing check waits until its deadline, and a published check holds until an admin or maintainer overrides it. Meanwhile the lane shows the job as passed or failed.\n   - The second key cannot forge a pass. It can still block, which the mark exists to prevent. That key might belong to another device of the checker member or be a compromised one.\n   - Make the lane refuse a room-source result whose signer key is not the job's marked key, or make the destination's refusal reopen the lane job. Add a Native witness: a result from a second key of the checker member arrives first, and the marked key's result then counts.\n   - I read this from the code and did not run it. If a test already shows otherwise, name it.\n2. **The implementation note does not meet the writing rule in AGENTS.md.** That rule asks for the technical-writing skill in every note, with no meta-voice and with clear sentences in context. The note is the record a later reader uses, and today it reads as compressed shorthand.\n   - Name things in full. Write \"Register 22, Directory 21, Rules 13, Destination 17, Issue 4 and Change 4\", as docs/architecture.md does, not \"R22/D21/Q13/G17\" and \"M8/I1\" (line 5).\n   - Replace the slashes with words throughout. Examples: \"member/role/action/controller/lifetime/clock eligibility\" (line 32) and \"own-head/replay/pre-recovery refusal\" (line 186).\n   - Restore the missing spaces: \"Node78\", \"Plain315/319files,3,086pass/4fail/1skip\" (line 212), \"historical76pins/40texts\" (line 207), \"Page grows219,279bytes(~23%);MCP296bytes\" (line 232) and \"Wall777.839s\" (line 225).\n   - Give commit IDs at least 10 hex digits. `7e` (line 147), `dcd` and `09ed` (line 211), `3713` (line 25), `bc601` and `335733` (line 205) are too short to resolve.\n   - Lines 15 to 23 are literal `Rests-On:` trailers inside the body. Replace them with a list of the rulings, each with one phrase on what it settles.\n   - 72 prose lines run past 79 characters. Line 241 runs to 143.\n   - Split the sentences that carry several facts. For example, \"Reasoned abandonment releases only the pin; fresh snapshots refuse it, while retained outcomes/identical retries/eligible late results survive\" (lines 64 and 65) needs three sentences.\n   - \"Base: Main `06e4d33f…`\" (line 7) was the starting point. The head now merges 8659af46, so name both.\n   - The witness list (lines 169 to 187) maps the design's 19 decisions to tests well. Keep its form.\n3. **The design note's status table still calls built parts open.** The `reuse` row and the `work` and `landing` stages row say \"Open\". The `published` stage and holds row says \"Partly met… The hold state is new\". This slice builds all three for the opt-in cohort, and the design-note status convention needs the table to match main once the head lands. Say what the first slice supplies and what stays open: audits above `every: 1`, holding while running, and `outside`.\n4. **The note claims a deployment authorization without naming it.** \"User authorized deployments; planner owns rollout\" (line 478) names no act, and the request asks for no deployment inference. Delete the first clause, or cite the act that authorizes a deployment.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n- **Stale bases.** Ruling 393b3b99 became stale when its question 346de78c was retired, ruling 1bc4f7fd when 2356b681 was retired, and ruling 31962e1f when 9ab75ec5 was retired. Every artifact rests on all three, so the review request was stale when it was filed. A stale base does not block a landing (planner 824623ad), but the recut is a good moment to fix it. Rest the new artifacts on live bases (a891555c, a53e1464 and c20152ef), and name the other rulings in the note's text.\n- **Pre-authorization reads (Security, low).** For member-source jobs, `destinationEvidenceAdmissionCurrent` returns true for any key (destination-evidence-v17.ts:218). So a job-read signed by an unrelated key starts the job read and up to 32 cross-scope source reads before the key check refuses it (stage-snapshot.ts:64-72, then :83). Check the key first, or say in entry-points.md that an ineligible key can trigger those reads.\n- **Re-marking the same key revives old passes for reuse.** The reuse path calls `evidenceSourceV17` without the generation check (reservation-v17.ts:74), and reuse keys do not include the generation (destination-evidence-v17.ts:631). After a mark of {M, K}, a withdrawal and a second mark of {M, K}, a pass from before the withdrawal becomes reusable again. That fits \"reuse tests current eligibility\". If a withdrawal is meant to revoke earlier passes, say so in the note, or add the generation to the check.\n- **Durable Object methods.** entry-points.md lists the new `{stage: true}` route but not the new object methods `stageSnapshot` and `workSourceState` (object.ts:927 and :942). It does not list the existing `jobState` either. If the page lists only routes, say so there.\n- **Replay window.** A captured job-read signature works for 15 minutes for anyone who holds it (stage-snapshot.ts:39-42). The older snapshot route has the same exposure, so this is not new.\n- **Compiled ledger at the head.** The note gives the destination@15 baseline, the discarded draft and one batch figure. One line for destination@17 as built (publication, branch and file publication) would show what legacy admission now counts against the 100,000-entry ceiling.\n- **Manifest size.** The manifest is 4,756,047 bytes, and main keeps it forever. The root SHA256SUMS digest already covers all 27,220 files. A manifest that lists each attempt's directory and its SHA256SUMS digest would carry the same proof in a small fraction of the size. That is a call for the planner under the PD-2 rule, not for this head.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe structure follows the design and its rulings:\n- Rules owns the nullable service mark and its generation.\n- Destination 17 admits jobs and results against the current Rules and Membership observations.\n- The lanes carry the jobs.\n- The holds are a judgment over recorded state at admission, with no timed port.\n\nThe new Destination reuses destination@15 through one injected policy port, `DestinationSuccessorV17`. So historical pins keep their code paths, and the new behaviour has one entry point. Rules observation revision 21 is the only kernel addition, and the binding types stay in platform. Item 1 is a flaw in how the lane and the destination agree on who decides a job, so changes requested.\n\n## Security\n\nThe read confirmed these, with file and line evidence:\n- Only an active admin marks or withdraws the checker (rules-scope-v13.ts:149-152). A custom publish permission does not suffice, and N1 shows it.\n- A result counts only with the exact marked key, from an active checker member with `change.check`, within its lifetime and with a fresh key read (reservation-v17.ts:49-74).\n- The signing key stays in the service process. The container runs with no network, a read-only root, no capabilities, no mounts and a scrubbed environment (container-engine.ts:161-164, container.ts:108).\n- The binding digest is domain-separated as `artroom-check-binding-1` and covers checker, source, stage, both generations, mark, `auditOf` and subject (evidence.ts:89-106).\n- Any mark change sets a new generation (rules-scope-v13.ts:168), so a reinstated mark does not revive a running job. N4 shows it.\n- Only a current pass clears a hold. Timeout, error and unknown outcomes only fail holds (destination-evidence-v17.ts:1077-1118).\n- Lists and records refuse when full instead of truncating.\n\nItem 1 lets a member's second key block a check, so changes requested.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe head adds about 5,600 lines of platform source for sources, stages, reuse, audits, holds, incidents and rotation. The recut after 393b3b99 removed 976 lines of byte-funding code, which was the right cut. The new modules match the density of the code around them (main's destination.ts has 222 lines over 160 characters). One port keeps the historical Destination unchanged, which is cheaper than a copied destination@17. Approved as to size. The manifest point above is the one place to save.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: changes requested (item 1).\n- Security: changes requested (item 1).\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's conditions:\n  - the historical definitions, pins, bytes and defaults, legacy entry admission, the mark, stage and subject provenance, the counted original carriers, literal TIME and the 19 witnesses are preserved, as far as I read them;\n  - the manifest's sha256 and the sealed 27,220-file inventory verify;\n  - I infer nothing about deployment.\n\nA recut that fixes item 1 needs the gate again, because the fix changes code. If items 2 to 4 change only documents, I'll check only the changed text and the fix for item 1.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3ad352d76ee5e01ba92a48097dfc01fdfc22ffc4","binding":"assigned","head":"9c15879a71dc761dee65d15b29c833cf5175605f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e7410aa67adb7f230b4999309ac713e62e6b8f7b\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a891555c470d4693e466b9c5240b5ed35a95b388\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e7410aa67adb7f230b4999309ac713e62e6b8f7b","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:04746d88c54a0f03a1e83a3d1ac64fbaeace1b0f, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:04f4b7b84a63f8cce5896724b006e02d592d39f5, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:07fd006572ec09c80d43a3ef9589ea8f242d04bd, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0a9713c58a135bc9cf0bd79e82d8b3c7fae7534a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0e7a428688ec386b93dd7877a2656